  <section id="mystery" class="mt-10">
    <h2 class="text-3xl md:text-4xl font-bold mb-3">The mystery: training looked perfect, evaluation didn't</h2>
    <p class="text-gray-700 mb-3">It began as one of those bugs that make you doubt your own tools. We evaluated a held-out retrieval metric every few hundred steps, and it would lurch — fine at one checkpoint, near-broken at the next, fine again — with no pattern anyone could see. So the first thing we did was stare at the training signals. They looked perfect. It looked like this — these three dashboards come from the minimal, CPU-runnable reproduction on synthetic data that the rest of this post builds, and they show the symptom exactly:</p>
    <figure class="bg-white rounded border p-2 mb-2">
      <img src="/plots/batchnorm-poisoning/toy_symptom.png" alt="Three panels from the reproduction: training loss smooth and converging, gradient norm with occasional spikes, and validation retrieval MRR crashing to near-zero at scattered checkpoints and recovering" class="w-full rounded" />
      <figcaption class="text-sm text-gray-500 px-2 pt-2">Fig. 1 — The symptom: loss and gradients look healthy while the eval metric crashes and recovers between checkpoints.</figcaption>
    </figure>
    <p class="text-gray-700 mb-3"><b>Top — training loss:</b> smooth and converging, <i>zero</i> spikes. <b>Middle — gradient norm:</b> a few spikes early on, but those are routine and easy to wave off. <b>Bottom — validation retrieval MRR:</b> the only thing that misbehaves — it crashes almost to zero at scattered checkpoints and pops back to its baseline at the next one, with no pattern. The loss you'd normally trust says everything is fine; only the eval, computed on separate checkpoints, betrays the problem — and it gives no clue as to <i>why</i>.</p>
    <p class="text-gray-700 mb-3">So we turned to the obvious suspect: the <i>evaluation code</i>. Maybe the metric itself was flaky — a data-loading race, a stale cache, nondeterminism in the retrieval index, an off-by-one in how pairs were scored. We had an AI coding agent read the <b>entire</b> eval pipeline end to end, hunting for anything that could make a good model look broken or score differently from one run to the next. It came back empty: the eval code was correct. The model really <i>was</i> collapsing at those checkpoints — and nothing we were logging explained it.</p>
  </section>

  <section id="breakthrough" class="mt-10">
    <h2 class="text-3xl md:text-4xl font-bold mb-3">The breakthrough: stop watching metrics, diff the checkpoints</h2>
    <p class="text-gray-700 mb-3">If the model trains fine but some saved checkpoints are broken, then <i>something must be physically different</i> about those checkpoints. The idea that finally cracked it (from the same AI agent, after the eval-code dead end) was almost too blunt to take seriously: stop staring at curves and just <b>diff the weights</b>. For every tensor in the model, measure how much it changed from a good checkpoint to the next, bad one — relative to its own size, <code>‖ΔW‖ / ‖W‖</code> — and sort.</p>
    <p class="text-gray-700 mb-3">One entry detonated off the chart — its relative change was about <b>3,000× larger</b> than the next tensor's (a five-order-of-magnitude span over the run). And it wasn't a weight at all: it was <code>first_conv.running_var</code>, a <b>buffer</b> inside the first BatchNorm layer — something gradient descent, and therefore the loss, never touches.</p>
    <p class="text-gray-700 mb-3">That single line is the whole bug. The minimal reproduction from the opening lets us watch it happen safely: diff two of <i>its</i> adjacent checkpoints tensor-by-tensor and the same fingerprint appears — one buffer towers over every learnable parameter:</p>
    <figure class="bg-white rounded border p-2 mb-2">
      <img src="/plots/batchnorm-poisoning/figure_weightdelta.png" alt="Bar chart of per-tensor relative weight change between two adjacent checkpoints; first_conv running_var towers over every other tensor" class="w-full rounded" />
      <figcaption class="text-sm text-gray-500 px-2 pt-2">Fig. 2 — Relative change per tensor between a good checkpoint and the next, bad one. One BatchNorm buffer dwarfs every learnable weight.</figcaption>
    </figure>
    <p class="text-gray-700 mb-3">Same story in miniature: every learnable parameter nudges by a fraction of a percent, while one BatchNorm buffer moves by hundreds of times its own size in a single checkpoint interval — over <b>1,700× more</b> than the next-largest tensor. The rest of this post is what that buffer is, why a few training samples send it flying, and how to fix it.</p>
  </section>

  <section id="hidden-buffer" class="mt-10">
    <h2 class="text-3xl md:text-4xl font-bold mb-3">The hidden buffer</h2>
    <p class="text-gray-700 mb-3"><a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/1502.03167" target="_blank" rel="noopener" class="text-blue-700 hover:underline">BatchNorm</a> does two different things depending on the mode. During <b>training</b> it standardizes each feature using the mean and variance of <i>the current mini-batch</i>. During <b>evaluation</b> it can't depend on whatever other examples happen to share the batch, so it instead uses a fixed pair of buffers — <code>running_mean</code> and <code>running_var</code> — that it accumulated during training as an exponential moving average:</p>
    <div class="bg-white rounded border p-4 mb-4 text-base md:text-lg leading-relaxed">
      <b>train step:</b> <code>x̂ = (x − μ<sub>batch</sub>) / √(σ²<sub>batch</sub> + ε)</code><br/>
      <b>buffer update:</b> <code>running_var ← (1−m)·running_var + m·σ²<sub>batch</sub></code><br/>
      <b>eval:</b> <code>x̂ = (x − running_mean) / √(running_var + ε)</code>
    </div>
    <div class="p-4 rounded border bg-amber-50 text-amber-900">
      <b>The crucial detail:</b> that buffer update is a plain moving average of batch statistics — it is <b>not driven by the gradient</b>. No loss term pushes on it. So the optimizer, the loss curve, and gradient-norm dashboards are all completely blind to it. One pathological batch can shove <code>running_var</code> orders of magnitude off, and nothing in training "notices."
    </div>
  </section>

  <section id="why-outliers" class="mt-10">
    <h2 class="text-3xl md:text-4xl font-bold mb-3">Why a few outliers poison it</h2>
    <p class="text-gray-700 mb-3">My point clouds are normalized by a <b>single dataset-wide divisor</b> rather than per-sample. That's deliberate: I want a 3&nbsp;mm part and a 4&nbsp;mm part to stay <i>different</i> after normalization, because absolute size is a real, discriminative feature. The cost is that there's <b>no per-sample clamp</b>: an object that is genuinely enormous (a building uploaded in millimeters, or a unit-error model) keeps enormous coordinates after "normalization".</p>
    <p class="text-gray-700 mb-3">The first convolution turns large coordinates into large activations, and variance grows with the <i>square</i> of the spread. The left panel below shows the distribution of per-sample extent (how far the coordinates reach after normalization) on a <b>log₁₀</b> axis: a healthy bulk at log₁₀ ≈ 0 (extent ≈ 1), with a detached tail of outliers out near log₁₀ ≈ 3–4 — that is, extent in the <b>thousands to ten-thousands</b>. The right panel plots the resulting first-layer variance against the raw extent (both axes log-scaled) — a clean <code>variance ∝ extent²</code> line, with the tail samples sitting at variances five to eight orders of magnitude above the bulk.</p>
    <figure class="bg-white rounded border p-2 mb-2">
      <img src="/plots/batchnorm-poisoning/data_diagnostic.png" alt="Left: histogram of per-sample extent with a long outlier tail. Right: first-conv variance grows as extent squared." class="w-full rounded" />
      <figcaption class="text-sm text-gray-500 px-2 pt-2">Fig. 3 — The driver: a detached tail of extreme-extent samples (left) whose first-layer variance grows as extent² (right).</figcaption>
    </figure>
    <p class="text-gray-700 mb-3">When one of those tail samples lands in a batch, the batch variance is dominated by it, the moving average drinks it in, and <code>running_var</code> spikes. At the next eval, every clean input gets divided by that inflated variance, the embedding collapses, and retrieval falls to chance — until enough clean batches pass for the moving average to recover. That's the see-saw.</p>
    <div class="p-4 rounded border bg-amber-50 text-amber-900">
      <b>But if the outlier is in a training batch, why doesn't the <i>loss</i> spike too?</b> Because real training runs on a <b>large effective batch</b> — gradient accumulation over many micro-batches, usually across several GPUs. The outlier lands in one micro-batch, so its contribution to the <i>averaged</i> gradient is diluted to near-nothing: the optimizer step, and the loss you log, barely budge. BatchNorm, though, computes its statistics <b>per micro-batch</b> — so that one micro-batch's variance is absorbed into <code>running_var</code> at full strength. <b>The averaging dilutes the outlier's gradient; it does not dilute its effect on the buffer.</b> That asymmetry is the whole reason the loss stays smooth while eval see-saws. (The reproduction above uses exactly this — micro-batch 36, accumulation 12 — which is why its top panel is smooth while its bottom panel crashes.)
    </div>
