  <section id="fixes" class="mt-10">
    <h2 class="text-3xl md:text-4xl font-bold mb-3">Three fixes, built one at a time</h2>
    <p class="text-gray-700 mb-3">I trained the same small encoder four ways — the bug, then three fixes. The point of showing all four isn't that the last one wins; it's that <b>each fix solves the previous one's problem and exposes a new one</b>. So I'll build them up in order. <code>global</code> means the shared-divisor normalization from above; <code>unitbb</code> means per-sample unit-box normalization (every object rescaled into the same cube). Here's the whole comparison at a glance, then one rung at a time:</p>

    <div class="overflow-x-auto mb-4">
      <table class="w-full text-sm md:text-base border-collapse">
        <thead><tr class="border-b text-left">
          <th class="py-2 pr-4">variant</th><th class="py-2 pr-4">stable?</th><th class="py-2 pr-4">knows scale?</th><th class="py-2">verdict</th>
        </tr></thead>
        <tbody class="text-gray-700">
          <tr class="border-b"><td class="py-2 pr-4"><code>global + BN</code></td><td class="py-2 pr-4">❌</td><td class="py-2 pr-4">yes</td><td class="py-2">the bug — running_var poisoned, eval swings 30 pts</td></tr>
          <tr class="border-b"><td class="py-2 pr-4"><code>global + GroupNorm</code></td><td class="py-2 pr-4">✅</td><td class="py-2 pr-4">partly</td><td class="py-2">no buffer, so no poisoning — already a big win</td></tr>
          <tr class="border-b"><td class="py-2 pr-4"><code>unitbb + GroupNorm</code></td><td class="py-2 pr-4">✅</td><td class="py-2 pr-4">❌ <b>no</b></td><td class="py-2">stable but scale-blind: can't tell 3 mm from 4 mm</td></tr>
          <tr><td class="py-2 pr-4"><code>unitbb + GroupNorm + FiLM</code></td><td class="py-2 pr-4">✅</td><td class="py-2 pr-4">✅ <b>yes</b></td><td class="py-2">stable <i>and</i> scale-aware — the full fix</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

    <figure class="bg-white rounded border p-2 mb-2">
      <img src="/plots/batchnorm-poisoning/four_variants.png" alt="Four-panel comparison: running_var, eval MRR, embedding-collapse indicator, and size discrimination across the four variants." class="w-full rounded" />
      <figcaption class="text-sm text-gray-500 px-2 pt-2">Fig. 5 — The four variants: <code>running_var</code>, eval MRR, embedding-collapse indicator, and 3&nbsp;mm-vs-4&nbsp;mm separation.</figcaption>
    </figure>

    <h3 id="fix-1" class="text-2xl font-bold mt-8 mb-2">Fix&nbsp;1 — GroupNorm: delete the poisoned buffer</h3>
    <p class="text-gray-700 mb-3"><b>What it does.</b> The bug lived in a shared, eval-time buffer, so the most direct fix is a normalization that has no such buffer. <a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/1803.08494" target="_blank" rel="noopener" class="text-blue-700 hover:underline"><b>GroupNorm</b></a> (orange) normalizes <i>within each sample</i> across channel groups — using only that sample's own statistics, at both train and eval. It keeps no running average of anything.</p>
    <p class="text-gray-700 mb-3"><b>Why it's a fix.</b> With no <code>running_var</code> to accumulate, there is nothing for an outlier batch to poison; train-mode and eval-mode compute the identical thing, and the see-saw disappears (orange, flat). It's a one-line layer swap and it's the fix that actually shipped. If all you need is stability, you can stop here.</p>

    <div class="p-4 rounded border bg-rose-50 text-rose-900 mb-3">
      <b>Still not perfect (1): it patches the propagation, not the source.</b> The outliers are still in the data, and a meter-scale part still produces enormous activations on its own forward pass. GroupNorm only stops that from leaking into a shared buffer — the occasional grad-norm spike remains, and any refactor that reintroduces a cross-sample statistic brings the bug straight back.
    </div>
    <div class="p-4 rounded border bg-rose-50 text-rose-900 mb-3">
      <b>Still not perfect (2): the scale it seems to keep is an accident.</b> Like every per-sample normalization (LayerNorm and InstanceNorm too), GroupNorm is <b>scale-invariant by construction</b>: <code>norm(s·x) = norm(x)</code>. So the 0.46 size-discrimination it shows above shouldn't exist at all — it survives only through a leak, which the next subsection pins down exactly. And the leak dies with the outlier fix: switch to per-sample <b>unit-box</b> normalization and a 3&nbsp;mm and a 4&nbsp;mm version of the same shape become <i>identical inputs before the network even sees them</i> — <code>unitbb + GroupNorm</code> (blue) scores <b>0.00</b> on telling them apart. Stable, but blind.
    </div>

    <h3 id="the-leak" class="text-2xl font-bold mt-8 mb-2">Why only <i>partly</i>? Where the leak comes from</h3>
    <p class="text-gray-700 mb-3">It's worth seeing exactly why <code>global + GroupNorm</code> keeps a <i>little</i> scale (0.46) when GroupNorm is supposedly scale-invariant. First, where would a scale signal even come from? Under global normalization a physically bigger object simply keeps larger coordinates — a 3&nbsp;mm part lands around ±0.05, a 4&nbsp;mm part around ±0.07 — so the sheer <i>magnitude</i> of each local patch is itself a proxy for absolute size. GroupNorm's whole job is to standardize that magnitude away, and it almost succeeds. What survives is a trace of <i>how big the patch is relative to a fixed reference inside the network</i>.</p>
    <p class="text-gray-700 mb-3">Here is that reference, precisely. The first conv produces, per channel, a <b>geometry part that scales with the object's size <code>s</code></b>, plus a <b>fixed offset <code>c</code></b> from the constant color (0.4) and the layer's bias — and crucially <code>c</code> is the <i>same</i> for a 3&nbsp;mm and a 4&nbsp;mm object (identical color). GroupNorm then z-scores each patch: subtract the group mean, divide by the group std. The whole question is whether <code>s</code> survives that. Follow it down each branch:</p>

    <div class="overflow-x-auto mb-3">
      <table class="w-full text-sm md:text-base border-collapse">
        <thead><tr class="border-b text-left">
          <th class="py-2 pr-4">GroupNorm step</th>
          <th class="py-2 pr-4">with the anchor &nbsp;<span class="font-normal text-gray-500">(real <code>global+GN</code>)</span></th>
          <th class="py-2">no anchor &nbsp;<span class="font-normal text-gray-500">(pure scaling)</span></th>
        </tr></thead>
        <tbody class="text-gray-700 align-top">
          <tr class="border-b"><td class="py-2 pr-4">conv output <code>v</code></td><td class="py-2 pr-4"><code>s·a + c</code></td><td class="py-2"><code>s·a</code></td></tr>
          <tr class="border-b"><td class="py-2 pr-4">− group mean</td><td class="py-2 pr-4"><code>s·(a − mean a) + (c − mean c)</code></td><td class="py-2"><code>s·(a − mean a)</code></td></tr>
          <tr class="border-b"><td class="py-2 pr-4">÷ group std</td><td class="py-2 pr-4"><code>÷ √( s²·Var(a) + 2s·Cov(a,c) + Var(c) )</code></td><td class="py-2"><code>÷ s·√Var(a)</code></td></tr>
          <tr class="border-b"><td class="py-2 pr-4">= normalized</td><td class="py-2 pr-4"><code>[ s·(a−mean a) + (c−mean c) ] / √( s²·Var(a) + 2s·Cov(a,c) + Var(c) )</code></td><td class="py-2"><code>(a − mean a) / √Var(a)</code></td></tr>
          <tr><td class="py-2 pr-4">depends on <code>s</code>?</td><td class="py-2 pr-4 text-rose-700"><b>yes → discriminable (0.46)</b></td><td class="py-2 text-emerald-700"><b>no — <code>s</code> cancels → blind (0.00)</b></td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

    <p class="text-gray-700 mb-3">The pivot is the last two rows. <b>No anchor:</b> numerator and denominator are <i>both</i> proportional to <code>s</code>, so it cancels exactly — every size yields the identical output. <b>With the anchor:</b> the numerator gains a constant term while the denominator becomes <code>√(s²·… + s·… + …)</code> — different powers of <code>s</code>, so it <i>can't</i> cancel. The normalized output then slides with size (toward the pure-geometry pattern when <code>s</code> is large, toward the anchor's pattern when <code>s</code> is small), and that slide is the entire 0.46. The fixed color isn't a scale signal because it <i>differs</i> between objects — it's identical — it's a non-scaling <b>ruler</b> the scaling geometry gets measured against.</p>

    <p class="text-gray-700 mb-3">And it's testable. Strip the anchor (color → 0, first-conv bias off, so the conv output is the pure <code>s·a</code> branch) and <code>global+GN</code>'s size discrimination collapses exactly as the algebra predicts — landing right on unit-box's numbers:</p>

    <div class="overflow-x-auto mb-3">
      <table class="w-full text-sm md:text-base border-collapse">
        <thead><tr class="border-b text-left"><th class="py-2 pr-4">variant</th><th class="py-2 pr-4">size-margin</th><th class="py-2">MRR</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody class="text-gray-700">
          <tr class="border-b"><td class="py-2 pr-4"><code>global+GN</code> — with anchor</td><td class="py-2 pr-4">0.46</td><td class="py-2">0.34</td></tr>
          <tr class="border-b"><td class="py-2 pr-4"><code>global+GN</code> — anchor removed</td><td class="py-2 pr-4"><b>0.00</b></td><td class="py-2">0.09</td></tr>
          <tr><td class="py-2 pr-4"><code>unitbb+GN</code> &nbsp;<span class="text-gray-500">(for comparison)</span></td><td class="py-2 pr-4"><b>0.00</b></td><td class="py-2">0.09</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

    <p class="text-gray-700 mb-3">Two routes to scale-blindness — remove the anchor, or remove scale at the input with unit-box — converge on the <i>same</i> numbers (the MRR drop to 0.09 is the family-only ceiling, since the teacher also rewards size the model can no longer see). So "partly" is a fragile by-product of a fixed reference, not a real scale representation. <span class="text-gray-500">(Reproduce: <code>ablation_anchor.py</code> in the repo.)</span> That fragility is what motivates the next two rungs: stop relying on the leak entirely — first strip scale cleanly at the input, then put it back as a signal we control.</p>

    <h3 id="fix-2" class="text-2xl font-bold mt-8 mb-2">Fix&nbsp;2 — Unit-box: remove the outliers at the source</h3>
    <p class="text-gray-700 mb-3"><b>What it does.</b> Replace the shared divisor with per-sample <b>unit-box</b> normalization (blue): rescale every object so its bounding box is the same cube, <i>before</i> the network sees it.</p>
    <p class="text-gray-700 mb-3"><b>Why it's a fix.</b> This goes after the root cause instead of the symptom. Once every sample is rescaled into the same box, there are no giant coordinates left <i>for anyone</i> — the outlier's extent is clamped to the same range as every clean part, so first-conv activations are bounded across the board. The explosion doesn't just fail to leak into a buffer; it never happens. Even with plain BatchNorm there'd be nothing to poison, and the grad-norm spikes go away too. It's the most robust rung against the mechanism, and it's the clean, scale-free geometry base we need for the next step.</p>
    <div class="p-4 rounded border bg-rose-50 text-rose-900 mb-3">
      <b>Still not perfect: it throws away a feature we need.</b> Unit-box makes a 3&nbsp;mm and a 4&nbsp;mm version of the same shape into <i>byte-identical inputs</i> — the encoder becomes truly scale-blind (size-margin <b>0.00</b>), and retrieval that leans on size collapses (MRR 0.34&nbsp;→&nbsp;0.10, because the teacher rewards a size the model can no longer see). For CAD, where an M3 and an M4 bolt are different parts, that's not acceptable. We fixed stability by amputating absolute scale.
    </div>

    <h3 id="fix-3" class="text-2xl font-bold mt-8 mb-2">Fix&nbsp;3 — Unit-box + FiLM: put scale back deliberately</h3>
    <p class="text-gray-700 mb-3"><b>What it does.</b> Keep unit-box + GroupNorm for the geometry (stable, scale-free), then re-inject absolute scale as an <b>explicit, dedicated signal</b> instead of letting it ride on raw activation magnitudes. Take the object's size, compress it with a log, expand it into <a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2006.10739" target="_blank" rel="noopener" class="text-blue-700 hover:underline">Fourier features</a> (so the network can resolve both tiny ratios like 3:4 and huge ones), pass it through a tiny MLP, and use the result to modulate the features — a <a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/1709.07871" target="_blank" rel="noopener" class="text-blue-700 hover:underline"><b>FiLM</b></a> layer (<code>output = (1+γ)·features + β</code>, with <code>γ, β</code> predicted from the size). It's the same trick <a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2212.09748" target="_blank" rel="noopener" class="text-blue-700 hover:underline">diffusion models use to inject the timestep</a>.</p>
    <figure class="bg-white rounded border p-2 mb-3">
      <div class="overflow-x-auto">
        <svg viewBox="0 0 1005 340" class="min-w-[760px] w-full" role="img" aria-label="Diagram: the point cloud splits into a scale-invariant shape path (unit-box then encoder) and an explicit scale path (log size, Fourier features, MLP), merged by a FiLM layer into a stable, size-aware embedding" font-family="ui-sans-serif, system-ui, sans-serif">
          <defs>
            <marker id="arr2" viewBox="0 0 10 10" refX="9" refY="5" markerWidth="7" markerHeight="7" orient="auto-start-reverse">
              <path d="M 0 0 L 10 5 L 0 10 z" fill="#64748b"/>
